\title{$p$-adic Berggren Dynamics:\\ The Tree of Pythagorean Triples as a Finite Dynamical System}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{21 August 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
The three Berggren (Barning--Hall) matrices generate the ternary tree that produces every
primitive Pythagorean triple exactly once, starting from $(3,4,5)$.  They are integral
isometries of the Lorentz form $q(a,b,c)=a^2+b^2-c^2$, and over $\R$ they realise a discrete
group of isometries of the hyperbolic plane: two parabolic (unipotent) generators and one
hyperbolic generator with eigenvalues $-1$ and $3\pm2\sqrt2$.  We study the reduction of this
system modulo $p^k$, that is, its $p$-adic dynamics.

We prove that each generator restricts to a bijection of the null cone modulo any modulus,
so the tree becomes a finite invertible dynamical system.  We determine its phase space
exactly: the null cone in $(\Z/p)^3$ has precisely $p^2$ points for every odd prime $p$,
hence $p^2-1$ nonzero points and $p+1$ null directions.  We show that the unipotent
generators have order exactly $p^k$ in $\GL_3(\Z/p^k)$ --- a pure $p$-power, the algebraic
signature of pro-$p$ behaviour --- and that this is sharp.  For the hyperbolic generator we
prove a Frobenius formula on its $2\times2$ block $U = 3+2J$ with $J^2=2$, deducing that
$\ord(B_2 \bmod p)$ divides $p-1$ when $2$ is a quadratic residue modulo $p$ and divides
$p+1$ otherwise; in all cases it divides $p^2-1$.  Equivalently, and by quadratic
reciprocity, $B_2$ possesses a nonzero eigenvector on the null cone if and only if
$p\equiv\pm1\pmod 8$: an exact split/inert dichotomy.  Modulo $p$ the hyperbolic generator
has no nonzero fixed vector, whereas each unipotent generator fixes an isotropic line of $p$
points.  A Hensel lifting argument gives $B_2^{(p^2-1)p^k}\equiv I \pmod{p^{k+1}}$, so every
point is periodic and $|B_2^{N}v-v|_p\le p^{-(k+1)}$ with $N=(p^2-1)p^k$: the hyperbolic
generator is periodic to arbitrary $p$-adic precision.  In sharp contrast with the real
picture, the reduced dynamics is never ergodic: every $B_2$-orbit has at most $p+1$ points,
so at least $p-1$ orbits are required.  At $p=2$ the system collapses completely, and this
degeneracy is exactly the classical parity structure of Pythagorean triples.  Finally, a
pigeonhole bound shows that the boundary of the tree cannot embed in any fixed finite level.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} Pythagorean triples, Berggren tree, Lorentz form, $p$-adic
dynamics, unipotent and hyperbolic matrices, quadratic reciprocity, Hensel lifting, null cone.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The tree of Pythagorean triples}

A \emph{Pythagorean triple} is a triple of positive integers $(a,b,c)$ with $a^2+b^2=c^2$; it
is \emph{primitive} if $\gcd(a,b,c)=1$.  Berggren (1934), rediscovered by Barning (1963) and
Hall (1970), observed that the three matrices
\[
B_1=\begin{pmatrix}1&-2&2\\ 2&-1&2\\ 2&-2&3\end{pmatrix},\qquad
B_2=\begin{pmatrix}1&2&2\\ 2&1&2\\ 2&2&3\end{pmatrix},\qquad
B_3=\begin{pmatrix}-1&2&2\\ -2&1&2\\ -2&2&3\end{pmatrix},
\]
acting on column vectors, generate from the root $(3,4,5)$ an infinite ternary tree
containing every primitive triple exactly once.  The first level is $(5,12,13)$, $(21,20,29)$,
$(15,8,17)$.

The structural reason is Lorentzian.  Define
\[
q(a,b,c) = a^2+b^2-c^2 ,
\]
the quadratic form of signature $(2,1)$.  A triple is Pythagorean precisely when it lies on
the \emph{null cone} $\{q=0\}$, and each $B_i$ preserves $q$.  Thus $\langle
B_1,B_2,B_3\rangle$ is a discrete subgroup of the integral orthogonal group
$\mathrm{O}(2,1;\Z)$, and the tree is one of its orbits on the cone.

The generators are of different geometric types.  Their characteristic polynomials are
\[
\chi_{B_1}(x)=\chi_{B_3}(x)=(x-1)^3, \qquad \chi_{B_2}(x)=(x+1)(x^2-6x+1),
\]
so $B_1$ and $B_3$ are unipotent (parabolic isometries of the hyperbolic plane) while $B_2$
is hyperbolic, with eigenvalues $-1$ and $3\pm2\sqrt2 = (1\pm\sqrt2)^2$, the square of the
fundamental unit of $\Z[\sqrt2]$.

\subsection{The question and a summary of results}

Reduction modulo $p^k$ transforms an infinite, freely branching, expansive system into a
finite one.  The questions that arise are quantitative: the size of the phase space, the
periods of the generators, the number of orbits, the persistence (or not) of the
unipotent/hyperbolic classification, and the fate of the boundary of the tree.  Our answers:

\begin{enumerate}
\item Each $B_i$ preserves $q$ over an arbitrary commutative ring and has an integral
inverse; hence each induces a bijection of the null cone modulo any modulus
(Theorems~\ref{thm:lorentz}, \ref{thm:bij}).
\item For odd $p$, the null cone in $(\Z/p)^3$ has exactly $p^2$ points, hence $p^2-1$
nonzero null vectors and $p+1$ null directions (Theorem~\ref{thm:census}).
\item $B_1=I+N_1$ with $N_1^3=0$; $\ord(B_1 \bmod p^k)=p^k$ exactly for odd $p$, and this is
sharp; the fixed set modulo $p$ is an isotropic line of $p$ points
(Theorems~\ref{thm:uniord}, \ref{thm:unifix}, Corollary~\ref{cor:sharp}).
\item $B_2$ is conjugate to $\diag(-1)\oplus U$ with $U=3+2J$, $J^2=2$; Frobenius gives
$U^p=3+2\cdot2^{(p-1)/2}J$, hence $\ord(B_2\bmod p) \mid p-1$ in the split case and $\mid
p+1$ in the inert case, always $\mid p^2-1$; $B_2$ has a nonzero null eigenvector iff
$p\equiv\pm1\pmod8$, and no nonzero fixed vector at all
(Theorems~\ref{thm:frob}--\ref{thm:nofix}).
\item $B_2^{(p^2-1)p^k}\equiv I \pmod{p^{k+1}}$, hence $|B_2^Nv-v|_p\le p^{-(k+1)}$ for all
integer $v$ (Theorems~\ref{thm:hensellift}, \ref{thm:contraction}).
\item Every $B_2$-orbit modulo $p$ has at most $p+1$ elements, so $B_2$ is never transitive
on the punctured null cone (Theorem~\ref{thm:noerg}).
\item All three generators are the identity modulo $2$; equivalently every vertex of the tree
is (odd, even, odd) (Theorems~\ref{thm:collapse}, \ref{thm:parity}).
\item If $3^d > p^2$, two distinct words of length $d$ have the same image modulo $p$
(Theorems~\ref{thm:coll1}, \ref{thm:coll2}).
\end{enumerate}

Throughout, ``odd prime'' means $p\neq2$.

\section{The system and its invariants}

Let $R$ be a commutative ring.  Regard $B_1,B_2,B_3\in\Mat_3(R)$ by reducing their integer
entries, and set
\[
q_R(v) = v_0^2+v_1^2-v_2^2,\qquad \cone(R) = \{v\in R^3 : q_R(v)=0\}.
\]

\begin{theorem}[Lorentz invariance]\label{thm:lorentz}
For every commutative ring $R$, every $i\in\{1,2,3\}$ and every $v\in R^3$ we have
$q_R(B_iv)=q_R(v)$.  The same holds for the inverses $B_i^{-1}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
A direct expansion.  For $B_2$, with $v=(a,b,c)$,
\[
(a+2b+2c)^2+(2a+b+2c)^2-(2a+2b+3c)^2 = a^2+b^2-c^2 ,
\]
an identity of polynomials with integer coefficients, hence valid in every commutative ring;
likewise for $B_1$ and $B_3$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Determinants and inverses]\label{thm:det}
$\det B_1=\det B_3=1$ and $\det B_2=-1$.  Explicitly
\[
B_1^{-1}=\begin{pmatrix}1&2&-2\\ -2&-1&2\\ -2&-2&3\end{pmatrix},\quad
B_2^{-1}=\begin{pmatrix}1&2&-2\\ 2&1&-2\\ -2&-2&3\end{pmatrix},\quad
B_3^{-1}=\begin{pmatrix}-1&-2&2\\ 2&1&-2\\ -2&-2&3\end{pmatrix},
\]
all with integer entries, so all three generators are units of $\Mat_3(R)$ for every $R$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Finite invertible dynamics]\label{thm:bij}
For every commutative ring $R$ and every $i$, the map $v\mapsto B_iv$ is a bijection of
$\cone(R)$ onto itself.  In particular, for $R=\Z/p^k$ each generator induces a permutation
of the finite set $\cone(\Z/p^k)$, and every point of the reduced system is periodic.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:lorentz} the map sends $\cone(R)$ into itself, and so does the map
induced by $B_i^{-1}$.  These two maps are mutually inverse, so each is a bijection of
$\cone(R)$.  Finiteness of $\cone(\Z/p^k)$ then forces periodicity.
\end{proof}

For a word $w=(i_1,\dots,i_d)$ in $\{1,2,3\}$ write $B_w = B_{i_1}\cdots B_{i_d}$, and let
$r=(3,4,5)$ be the root.

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:tree}
For every word $w$ and every commutative ring $R$, $q_R(B_wr)=0$: the whole tree lies on the
null cone.
\end{proposition}

\section{The phase space: counting the null cone}

\begin{theorem}[Null-cone census]\label{thm:census}
Let $p$ be an odd prime.  Then $\#\cone(\Z/p)=p^2$.  Consequently there are $p^2-1$ nonzero
null vectors and $(p^2-1)/(p-1)=p+1$ null directions.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Fibre the cone over the light-cone coordinate $u=v_2-v_0$.  We show every fibre has exactly
$p$ points.

Suppose $u\neq0$.  On the cone, $v_1^2 = v_2^2-v_0^2 = (v_2-v_0)(v_2+v_0) = u(v_2+v_0)$.
Since $u$ is invertible, $v_2+v_0 = v_1^2u^{-1}$ is determined by $v_1$; combined with
$v_2-v_0=u$ and the invertibility of $2$, both $v_0$ and $v_2$ are determined by $v_1$.
Conversely, for each $v_1=b$ the vector
\[
\Bigl(\tfrac12\bigl(b^2u^{-1}-u\bigr),\; b,\; \tfrac12\bigl(b^2u^{-1}+u\bigr)\Bigr)
\]
lies on the cone and in the fibre.  So the fibre is a graph over $\Z/p$ and has $p$ points.

If $u=0$, i.e.\ $v_2=v_0$, then $q(v)=v_1^2=0$ forces $v_1=0$, and the fibre is the isotropic
line $\{(s,0,s)\}$, again with $p$ points.  There are $p$ fibres, so $\#\cone(\Z/p)=p^2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
The uniformity of the fibres --- including the degenerate one --- reflects the nondegeneracy
and isotropy of $q$: the projective conic $q=0$ in $\mathbb{P}^2(\F_p)$ is smooth with $p+1$
rational points, each contributing $p-1$ nonzero vectors, plus the origin: $(p+1)(p-1)+1=p^2$.
\end{remark}

\section{The unipotent generators}

\begin{lemma}[Nilpotent decomposition]\label{lem:nil}
$B_1=I+N_1$ and $B_3=I+N_3$ with
\[
N_1=\begin{pmatrix}0&-2&2\\ 2&-2&2\\ 2&-2&2\end{pmatrix},\qquad
N_3=\begin{pmatrix}-2&2&2\\ -2&0&2\\ -2&2&2\end{pmatrix},
\]
and
\[
N_1^2=\begin{pmatrix}0&0&0\\ 0&-4&4\\ 0&-4&4\end{pmatrix},\quad N_1^3=0,\qquad
N_3^2=\begin{pmatrix}-4&0&4\\ 0&0&0\\ -4&0&4\end{pmatrix},\quad N_3^3=0 .
\]
Moreover $N_1^2=0$ in $\Mat_3(R)$ if and only if $4=0$ in $R$: the nilpotency index is $3$ at
every odd prime power and drops to $2$ modulo $2$ and modulo $4$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}[Unipotent power formula]\label{lem:powunip}
In any ring, if $X^3=0$ then $(I+X)^n = I+nX+\binom n2 X^2$ for all $n\ge0$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Induction on $n$, using $\binom{n+1}{2}=\binom n2 + n$ and $X^3=0$ to kill the only surviving
cross term.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exact order of the unipotent generators]\label{thm:uniord}
Let $p$ be an odd prime and $k\ge1$.  Then $B_1^{p^k}=B_3^{p^k}=I$ in $\GL_3(\Z/p^k)$, and
conversely $B_1^{m}=I$ in $\GL_3(\Z/p^k)$ implies $p^k\mid m$.  Hence
$\ord(B_1\bmod p^k)=p^k$ exactly: a pure $p$-power, with no prime-to-$p$ part.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Lemmas~\ref{lem:nil} and \ref{lem:powunip}, $B_1^{n} = I+nN_1+\binom n2 N_1^2$.  Take
$n=p^k$: then $n\equiv0\pmod{p^k}$, and $\binom{p^k}{2}=p^k\cdot\frac{p^k-1}{2}$ is an
integer multiple of $p^k$ because $p^k$ is odd; both corrections vanish.  The same applies to
$B_3$.

Conversely, the $(0,1)$ entry of $I+mN_1+\binom m2N_1^2$ equals $-2m$, since $(N_1)_{01}=-2$
and $(N_1^2)_{01}=0$.  Thus $B_1^m=I$ forces $2m\equiv0\pmod{p^k}$; as $\gcd(p^k,2)=1$, we
get $p^k\mid m$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Sharpness of depth]\label{cor:sharp}
For odd $p$ and $k\ge0$, $B_1^{p^k}=I$ modulo $p^k$ but $B_1^{p^k}\neq I$ modulo $p^{k+1}$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Fixed sets of the unipotent generators]\label{thm:unifix}
Over any commutative ring $B_1(0,1,1)^{\top}=(0,1,1)^{\top}$ and
$B_3(1,0,1)^{\top}=(1,0,1)^{\top}$, and both fixed vectors are null.  Modulo an odd prime
$p$, the fixed set of $B_1$ is exactly the isotropic line $\{(0,t,t)\}$, of cardinality $p$;
symmetrically for $B_3$ with $\{(t,0,t)\}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The first assertion is a direct multiplication, and $q(0,t,t)=0$.  For the classification,
$B_1v=v$ reads $N_1v=0$, i.e.\ $-2v_1+2v_2=0$ and $2v_0-2v_1+2v_2=0$ (twice).  As $2$ is
invertible, the first gives $v_1=v_2$ and the second then $v_0=0$.
\end{proof}

\section{The hyperbolic generator modulo $p$}

\begin{lemma}[Block decomposition]\label{lem:block}
Let
\[
W=\begin{pmatrix}1&1&0\\ -1&1&0\\ 0&0&1\end{pmatrix},\qquad
S=\diag(-1,1,1),\qquad
\iota(U)=\begin{pmatrix}1&0&0\\ 0&3&2\\ 0&4&3\end{pmatrix},\qquad
U=\begin{pmatrix}3&2\\4&3\end{pmatrix}.
\]
Then $B_2W = W\,(S\,\iota(U))$ over every commutative ring, and
$W\cdot\left(\begin{smallmatrix}1&-1&0\\1&1&0\\0&0&2\end{smallmatrix}\right)=2I$, so $W$ is
invertible whenever $2$ is.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}[The block is $3+2\sqrt2$]\label{lem:sqrt2}
Set $J=\left(\begin{smallmatrix}0&1\\2&0\end{smallmatrix}\right)$.  Then $J^2=2I$ and
$U=3I+2J$ with $\det U=9-8=1$, equivalently $(3+2J)(3-2J)=I$.  Thus $U$ realises
multiplication by the norm-one unit $3+2\sqrt2=(1+\sqrt2)^2$ of $\Z[\sqrt2]$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Frobenius on the hyperbolic block]\label{thm:frob}
Let $p$ be an odd prime.  In $\Mat_2(\Z/p)$ we have $U^p = 3I + 2\cdot2^{(p-1)/2}J$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Scalars commute with everything, so in characteristic $p$, $(3I+2J)^p=(3I)^p+(2J)^p$.  By
Fermat, $(3I)^p=3I$ and $(2J)^p=2J^p$.  Since $p$ is odd,
$J^p=(J^2)^{(p-1)/2}J=2^{(p-1)/2}J$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Period of the hyperbolic generator]\label{thm:period}
Let $p$ be an odd prime.
\begin{enumerate}
\item If $2$ is a quadratic residue modulo $p$, then $B_2^{\,p-1}=I$ in $\GL_3(\Z/p)$.
\item If $2$ is not a quadratic residue modulo $p$, then $B_2^{\,p+1}=I$.
\item In all cases $B_2^{\,p^2-1}=I$, i.e.\ $\ord(B_2\bmod p)\mid p^2-1$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Euler's criterion $2^{(p-1)/2}=\pm1$ according to the quadratic character of $2$.

\emph{Split case.}  $2^{(p-1)/2}=1$ gives $U^p=3I+2J=U$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:frob}, and
multiplying by $U^{-1}$ (which exists since $\det U=1$) yields $U^{p-1}=I$.  The sign block
satisfies $S^{p-1}=I$ because $p-1$ is even.  Conjugating back through
Lemma~\ref{lem:block} --- legitimate as $2$ is invertible modulo $p$ --- gives $B_2^{p-1}=I$.

\emph{Inert case.}  $2^{(p-1)/2}=-1$ gives $U^p=3I-2J=U^{-1}$, whence $U^{p+1}=I$, and
$S^{p+1}=I$ since $p+1$ is even; conjugating back gives $B_2^{p+1}=I$.

Finally $p^2-1=(p-1)(p+1)$ is a multiple of both exponents.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Null eigenvectors and $p\bmod8$]\label{thm:dichotomy}
Let $p$ be an odd prime.  The following are equivalent:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $B_2$ has a nonzero eigenvector lying on the null cone modulo $p$;
\item $2$ is a square modulo $p$;
\item $p\equiv\pm1\pmod8$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$(2)\Rightarrow(1)$: if $s^2=2$ in $\Z/p$ then $v=(1,1,s)$ satisfies $q(v)=1+1-s^2=0$ and
$B_2v=(3+2s)v$, with $(3+2s)(3-2s)=9-4s^2=1$, so the eigenvalue is a unit.

$(1)\Rightarrow(2)$: by Lemma~\ref{lem:block} the eigenvalues of $B_2$ are $-1$, with
eigenvector $(1,-1,0)$ satisfying $q=2\neq0$, and the eigenvalues $3\pm2s$ of $U$, which are
$\Z/p$-rational only if $s^2=2$ is solvable.  A null eigenvector is not proportional to
$(1,-1,0)$, so its eigenvalue lies in the $U$-block.

$(2)\Leftrightarrow(3)$: the supplementary law of quadratic reciprocity,
$\left(\tfrac2p\right)=(-1)^{(p^2-1)/8}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[No nonzero fixed vector]\label{thm:nofix}
Let $p$ be an odd prime and $v\in(\Z/p)^3$ with $B_2v=v$.  Then $v=0$.  Consequently, modulo
$p$ the unipotent generator has exactly $p$ fixed points and the hyperbolic generator exactly
one.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Coordinatewise, $B_2v=v$ gives $2(v_1+v_2)=0$, $2(v_0+v_2)=0$, $2(v_0+v_1+v_2)=0$.  As $2$ is
invertible, subtracting the first two from the third yields $v_2=0$, then $v_0=v_1=0$.  The
count for $B_1$ is Theorem~\ref{thm:unifix}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Genuine noncommutativity]\label{thm:noncomm}
For every odd prime $p$, $B_1B_2\neq B_2B_1$ in $\Mat_3(\Z/p)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The $(0,0)$ entries are $1$ and $9$; their difference $8$ vanishes modulo $p$ only for $p=2$.
\end{proof}

\section{Depth: lifting to $\Z/p^k$}

Call an integer matrix $A$ \emph{entrywise divisible} by $d$ if $d$ divides every entry.

\begin{lemma}[Hensel step]\label{lem:henselstep}
Let $A\in\Mat_3(\Z)$, $p$ prime and $j\ge1$.  If $A-I$ is entrywise divisible by $p^j$, then
$A^p-I$ is entrywise divisible by $p^{j+1}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Set $X=A-I$.  Expanding, $A^p-I = pX + X^2C$ for some integer matrix $C$ (formally
$(I+X)^n=I+nX+X^2C_n$ by induction).  The first summand is divisible by $p\cdot p^j$, the
second by $p^{2j}$, and $2j\ge j+1$ since $j\ge1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Hensel lift]\label{lem:hensellift}
If $A-I$ is entrywise divisible by $p$, then $A^{p^k}-I$ is entrywise divisible by $p^{k+1}$
for all $k\ge0$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[$p$-adic periodicity of the hyperbolic generator]\label{thm:hensellift}
For every odd prime $p$ and every $k\ge0$,
\[
B_2^{\,(p^2-1)p^k} = I \quad\text{in } \GL_3(\Z/p^{k+1}).
\]
In particular every point of $(\Z/p^{k+1})^3$, and hence of the null cone at depth $k+1$, is
periodic with period dividing $(p^2-1)p^k$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Theorem~\ref{thm:period}(3) says $p$ divides $B_2^{p^2-1}-I$ entrywise over $\Z$; apply
Lemma~\ref{lem:hensellift} to $A=B_2^{p^2-1}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[$p$-adic contraction]\label{thm:contraction}
Let $p$ be an odd prime, $k\ge0$, $N=(p^2-1)p^k$ and $v\in\Z^3$ arbitrary.  Then every
coordinate of $B_2^{N}v-v$ is divisible by $p^{k+1}$; equivalently
$|B_2^{N}v-v|_p\le p^{-(k+1)}$.  Similarly $|B_1^{p^k}v-v|_p\le p^{-k}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Entrywise divisibility of $B_2^N-I$ passes to matrix--vector products.  For $B_1$,
Lemma~\ref{lem:powunip} gives $B_1^{p^k}-I = p^kN_1+\binom{p^k}{2}N_1^2$, and both
coefficients are divisible by $p^k$ by the proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:uniord}.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
This is the precise sense in which the hyperbolic generator, which expands without bound at
the archimedean place, is $p$-adically recurrent: its expansion factor $3+2\sqrt2$ is a
$p$-adic unit.  For $p=5$, $k=2$, $N=600$, the vector $B_2^{600}(3,4,5)$ has $460$-digit
entries yet agrees with $(3,4,5)$ to three $5$-adic digits.
\end{remark}

\section{Orbit structure and the failure of ergodicity}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:mod}
If $M^m=I$ then $M^n=M^{n\bmod m}$ for all $n$; hence every forward orbit has at most $m$
elements.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Short orbits; no transitivity]\label{thm:noerg}
Let $p$ be an odd prime.  There is $m$ with $1\le m\le p+1$ and $B_2^m=I$ in $\GL_3(\Z/p)$;
consequently every $B_2$-orbit in $(\Z/p)^3$ has at most $p+1$ points.  Since the punctured
null cone has $p^2-1=(p-1)(p+1)$ points and $p\ge3$, no single orbit exhausts it: $B_2$ is
never transitive on the nonzero null vectors, and at least $p-1$ orbits are needed.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Take $m=p-1$ in the split case, $m=p+1$ in the inert case (Theorem~\ref{thm:period}); both
are at most $p+1$, and Lemma~\ref{lem:mod} bounds orbits by $m$.  Transitivity would give
$p^2-1\le p+1$, false for $p\ge3$; dividing cardinality by the maximal orbit length gives the
bound $p-1$ on the number of orbits.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Explicit orbits in the split case]\label{thm:eigorbit}
Suppose $s^2=2$ in $\Z/p$ and let $v=(1,1,s)$.  Then $B_2^{n}v=(3+2s)^nv$ for all $n\ge0$,
and $B_2^{n}v=v$ if and only if $(3+2s)^n=1$.  Hence the exact period of $B_2$ on this null
line is the multiplicative order of $3+2\sqrt2$ in $(\Z/p)^\times$.
\end{theorem}

