\section{The prime $2$: total collapse and the parity of the tree}

\begin{theorem}[Collapse at $2$]\label{thm:collapse}
In $\Mat_3(\Z/2)$ we have $B_1=B_2=B_3=I$.  Consequently every word in the generators reduces
to $I$ modulo $2$, and every vertex of the tree satisfies $B_wr\equiv r\equiv(1,0,1)\pmod2$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Every off-diagonal entry of each $B_i$ is even and every diagonal entry is odd; the statement
about words follows by induction on the length.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Parity of the tree]\label{thm:parity}
For every word $w$, the triple $B_wr=(a,b,c)$ satisfies $a\equiv1$, $b\equiv0$, $c\equiv1
\pmod2$.  In particular no vertex of the tree has two odd legs.
\end{theorem}

\begin{remark}
The classical parity constraint on primitive Pythagorean triples --- exactly one leg even ---
is thus a fixed-point theorem for the $2$-adic dynamics.  Degeneracy at $2$ and arithmetic
rigidity at $2$ are the same phenomenon.  Consistently, Lemma~\ref{lem:nil} shows the
nilpotency index of $N_1$ drops from $3$ to $2$ exactly when $4=0$.
\end{remark}

\section{The boundary of the tree does not survive reduction}

\begin{theorem}[Collision bound]\label{thm:coll1}
Let $m\ge1$ and $d\ge1$ with $m^3<3^d$.  Then there are distinct words $w_1\neq w_2$ of
length $d$ with $B_{w_1}r\equiv B_{w_2}r \pmod m$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The word map sends $3^d$ words into $(\Z/m)^3$, of size $m^3<3^d$; pigeonhole.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Quadratic collision bound on the cone]\label{thm:coll2}
Let $p$ be an odd prime and $d$ with $3^d>p^2$.  Then two distinct words of length $d$ have
the same image modulo $p$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
All images lie on the null cone modulo $p$ (Proposition~\ref{prop:tree}), which has exactly
$p^2$ points (Theorem~\ref{thm:census}); pigeonhole.
\end{proof}

Thus no fixed level $\Z/p^k$ can hold a faithful copy of the boundary, and the box dimension
of the reduction is at most $2$.  For $p=5$, $d=4$, the $81$ words of length $4$ produce only
$12$ distinct residues.  What survives is the inverse-limit statement of the previous
section: the generators are periodic to any prescribed precision, so the system is
well-defined and nontrivial over $\Z_p$ even though every finite truncation is a small finite
permutation group.

\section{Algorithms}

\begin{description}
\item[A. Order of a generator modulo $m$.]  Iterate powers of $B$ modulo $m$ until the
identity is reached: $O(\ord(B))$ matrix multiplications.  Alternatively verify a candidate
exponent $N$ by binary exponentiation in $O(\log N)$ multiplications; for $B_1$ the answer is
provably $p^k$, and for $B_2$ modulo $p$ it divides $p\mp1$ according to
$\left(\tfrac2p\right)$.
\item[B. Null-cone generation.]  Naive enumeration is $O(p^3)$; the fibration of
Theorem~\ref{thm:census} generates the cone in $O(p^2)$ by emitting
$\bigl(\tfrac12(b^2u^{-1}-u),b,\tfrac12(b^2u^{-1}+u)\bigr)$ for each unit $u$ and each $b$,
plus the isotropic line $(s,0,s)$.
\item[C. Orbit decomposition.]  Mark-and-walk over the punctured cone, cost $O(p^2)$ vector
multiplications, returning the cycle type of the induced permutation.
\item[D. Hensel verification.]  Binary exponentiation modulo $p^{k+1}$ to confirm
$B_2^{(p^2-1)p^k}\equiv I$, in $O(\log((p^2-1)p^k))$ multiplications of $3\times3$ matrices
with entries of size $O(k\log p)$.
\end{description}

\section{Discussion and open problems}

The reduction preserves the \emph{classification} and destroys the \emph{dynamics}.
Preserved: unipotence with a pure $p$-power order and an isotropic fixed line; the hyperbolic
block $3+2\sqrt2$ and its norm-one character; noncommutativity; invariance of $q$;
invertibility.  Lost: infinite order, expansion, and above all mixing.  The genuinely new
local phenomenon is the split/inert dichotomy of Theorem~\ref{thm:dichotomy}: the hyperbolic
generator is hyperbolic modulo $p$ exactly for $p\equiv\pm1\pmod8$ and elliptic otherwise.

\begin{conjecture}[Null cone at depth $k$]
For every odd prime $p$ and every $k\ge1$,
\[
\#\{v\in(\Z/p^k)^3 : v_0^2+v_1^2-v_2^2=0\}
 = (p-1)p^{2k-1}\sum_{j=0}^{k-1}p^{-\lceil j/2\rceil} + p^{\,2k-\lceil k/2\rceil},
\]
which for $k=1$ is $p^2$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:census}) and for $k=2$ is $p^4+p^3-p^2$ (verified
by enumeration for $p=3$, value $99$, and $p=5$, value $725$).  The substitution $u=c-a$,
$v=c+a$ turns the condition into $uv=b^2$, so the count is governed by the valuation of $u$;
the valuation-$j$ strata, i.e.\ the singular locus of the cone, break the naive guess
$p^{2k}$.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{remark}[The $p$-part of the period is \emph{not} always sharp]
One would guess that the order of $B_2$ in $\GL_3(\Z/p^{k+1})$ is exactly
$\ord(B_2\bmod p)\cdot p^k$, making Theorem~\ref{thm:hensellift} sharp in its $p$-part; the
mechanism would be $B_2^{\ord(B_2\bmod p)}=I+pN$ with $N\not\equiv0\pmod p$, so that the
pro-$p$ subgroup generated is torsion-free of level exactly one.  The unipotent analogue is
Corollary~\ref{cor:sharp}, a theorem, and for $p=3,5,7$ the orders modulo $p,p^2,p^3$ are
indeed $(4,12,36)$, $(6,30,150)$, $(6,42,294)$.  Nevertheless the guess is false: computation
shows that $p=13$ and $p=31$ are exceptions, and the only ones below $3000$,
\[
\ord(B_2\bmod 13)=\ord(B_2\bmod 13^2)=14,\qquad \ord(B_2\bmod 13^3)=182=14\cdot13,
\]
\[
\ord(B_2\bmod 31)=\ord(B_2\bmod 31^2)=30,\qquad \ord(B_2\bmod 31^3)=930=30\cdot31 .
\]
These are Wieferich-type primes for the unit $3+2\sqrt2$: the eigenvalue is already trivial to
two $p$-adic digits, so the first Hensel step buys no new period.
\end{remark}

\begin{conjecture}[Corrected sharpness]
For every odd prime $p$ there is a level $w_p\ge1$, the Wieferich level of $3+2\sqrt2$ at $p$,
with
\[
\ord\bigl(B_2\bmod p^{k}\bigr)=\ord(B_2\bmod p)\cdot p^{\max(0,\,k-w_p)} ,
\]
and $w_p=1$ for every $p<3000$ except $w_{13}=w_{31}=2$.  Whether $w_p$ is bounded, and
whether the exceptional set is infinite, appear to be as hard as the classical questions about
Wieferich primes.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Orbit count of the hyperbolic generator]
Determine, as an explicit function of $p$ and $p\bmod8$, the number of $B_2$-orbits on the
null cone modulo $p$.  Theorem~\ref{thm:noerg} gives the lower bound $p-1$; the computed
counts are $2,4,10,10,12,36$ for $p=3,5,7,11,13,17$.  A formula should follow from the
split/inert division together with the cycle structure of multiplication by $3+2\sqrt2$ on
the $p+1$ null directions.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{problem}[A profinite boundary]
Theorems~\ref{thm:coll1}--\ref{thm:coll2} exclude a Cantor set at any fixed finite level.  Is
there a natural injection of the boundary of the tree into $\Z_p^3$, or into the projective
null cone over $\Z_p$, carrying a computable Hausdorff dimension?  The correct setting is the
inverse limit over $k$.
\end{problem}

\begin{problem}[Local--global depth]
Call a primitive triple \emph{$p$-adically deep} if it is close to $(3,4,5)$ in the $p$-adic
metric.  Which triples are deep, and how does $p$-adic depth correlate with depth in the
tree?
\end{problem}

\begin{problem}[Other quadratic forms]
The analysis used only that $q$ is a nondegenerate isotropic ternary form and that the
hyperbolic generator is the matrix of a norm-one unit of a real quadratic order.  Everything
should generalise to trees of solutions of $a^2+Db^2=c^2$, with ``$2$ is a square modulo
$p$'' replaced by a splitting condition for $p$ in $\Q(\sqrt D)$.
\end{problem}

\section{Conclusion}

The Berggren tree, reduced modulo a prime power, is a completely analysable finite dynamical
system.  Its phase space has exactly $p^2$ points; its two parabolic generators have order
exactly $p^k$ at depth $k$ and each fixes one isotropic line; its hyperbolic generator has
order dividing $p-1$ or $p+1$ according as $p\equiv\pm1$ or $\pm3\pmod8$, always dividing
$p^2-1$, and fixes nothing but the origin.  Lifting by Hensel's principle, the hyperbolic
generator is periodic to arbitrary $p$-adic precision, with period dividing $(p^2-1)p^k$
modulo $p^{k+1}$.  Yet its orbits are short: the system is never transitive on the null cone,
so the mixing of the real hyperbolic picture is entirely lost.  At $p=2$ the system
degenerates to a point --- and that degeneracy is exactly the classical parity of Pythagorean
triples.  The infinite ternary boundary cannot be embedded at any fixed finite level; what
remains, and what invites further work, is the profinite limit.

